PG.notes("l248", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why PostgreSQL is the course's database, where a database sits in the client / server / app architecture, and previews the node-postgres (pg) code pattern used to talk to it from Node. Ends by naming the two programs to install next: the Postgres server and pgAdmin.</p>\n<h3>Why Postgres</h3>\n<ul><li>Postgres is a relational database management system (DBMS) used to persist app data permanently, beyond a single request or server restart.</li><li>Reasons given for choosing it: widely used by large companies (Apple, Twitch, Instagram, NASA are named), free and open source, strong career value, and a big community so help is easy to find.</li><li>Its own tagline is that it is the most advanced open source relational database. 'Relational' means data lives in tables that can reference each other (covered later with keys and joins).</li></ul>\n<h3>Where the database fits in the architecture</h3>\n<ul><li>Flow: client (front-end in the browser) sends a request, the server-side app (index.js / app.js) applies business logic, and that code talks to the database.</li><li>The database stores anything that must outlive a request: user records, emails, password hashes, blog posts, to-do items, social 'likes'.</li><li>Example flow: a request for a blog post from a given month makes the app query the database for that row, then format it and respond with the right status code for the client to display.</li><li>Mental model: the database is its own server process, like the API server and website server from earlier sections that ran on separate localhost ports. It can run on your own machine (this course) or be hosted in the cloud.</li></ul>\n<h3>Connecting from Node with the pg package</h3>\n<ul><li>The npm package is node-postgres, installed under the name pg. It is the standard way to use Postgres from Node.</li><li>A db object (a pg Client) is created with the connection details: user, host, database name, password and port. These authenticate you and select which database receives your data.</li><li>db.connect() opens the connection to the Postgres server.</li><li>db.query(sqlString, callback) runs SQL passed as a plain string. This is the method used for almost all database work in the course.</li><li>The callback receives an error (handle it if present) and a result; the returned records are in result.rows, an array of row objects.</li><li>db.end() closes the connection when finished.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer pg versions also return a promise from query(), so async/await works. Later lectures use this style.</li></ul>\n<h3>What to install next</h3>\n<ul><li>Postgres server: the actual database engine that runs on your computer, so no cloud storage fees.</li><li>pgAdmin: a graphical client for the server, so you can browse, organise and query data visually instead of using command-line tools.</li><li>Installation steps are in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Overview pattern for querying Postgres from Node (illustrative; values are placeholders)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;world&quot;,\n  password: &quot;your-password&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\n\ndb.connect();\n\ndb.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users&quot;, (err, res) =&gt; {\n  if (err) {\n    console.error(&quot;Error executing query&quot;, err.stack);\n  } else {\n    console.log(res.rows);\n  }\n  db.end();\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Call db.end() only after queries finish; ending early (outside the callback) can cut off the query.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Do not commit real database passwords to a public repo; use environment variables.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l249", "<p class=\"summary\">Step-by-step install of the Postgres server together with pgAdmin on Windows or macOS using the EnterpriseDB installer.</p>\n<h3>Installation steps</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the installer for your OS (separate Windows and Mac links are given in the lesson) and double-click it.</li><li>Allow the installer to run: choose Yes on the Windows permission prompt; on a Mac choose Open and enter your Mac password if asked.</li><li>Click Next through the wizard until the component list, and make sure everything is ticked, pgAdmin in particular (it is the GUI used in the following lessons).</li><li>At the password screen the superuser name is postgres. Pick a password and write it down: it goes into the db connection config in Node later.</li><li>Leave all other options at their defaults, click Next until done, then Finish.</li><li>If Stack Builder opens after install, close it; the course does not use it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Defaults worth remembering</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The default port is 5432 and the host is localhost; both go into the pg client config along with user postgres and your password.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The notes here are based on the lesson's text as mirrored in the reference repo, because the article body opens in a separate window and could not be read inline.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the superuser password set during install; it cannot be skipped and is needed for pgAdmin and Node.</li><li>Leaving pgAdmin unticked in the component list, so no GUI is installed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l250", "<p class=\"summary\">Recaps tables, columns and rows, then shows how a table is declared in Postgres: PRIMARY KEY, SERIAL auto-increment, and the common column types VARCHAR, CHAR, TEXT, INT and BOOLEAN, using a friends table as the running example.</p>\n<h3>Tables, columns and rows</h3>\n<ul><li>A column is a vertical field (name, age, ...) that describes one characteristic of the data. A row is a horizontal record, one entry such as one friend.</li><li>A database is a set of tables linked by relationships and rules for how they behave.</li><li>Compared with spreadsheets, databases stay fast with huge record counts (millions of rows), whereas a spreadsheet gets sluggish after a few thousand.</li></ul>\n<h3>CREATE TABLE in Postgres</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: CREATE TABLE table_name ( field_name type constraints, ... ); with the whole definition ending in a semicolon.</li><li>Each field is written as its name, then its data type, then optional keywords. Fields are separated by commas inside the parentheses.</li><li>Postgres has a larger set of types than the basic SQL ones seen earlier.</li></ul>\n<h3>PRIMARY KEY and SERIAL</h3>\n<ul><li>PRIMARY KEY marks the column whose values must be unique and which uniquely identifies each record. Two friends can share a name, so a name is not a safe way to pick out one row; an id is.</li><li>SERIAL is a Postgres type for auto-incrementing integers: when inserting a new row you leave id out and Postgres gives it the next number (previous + 1).</li><li>id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY is therefore the standard first column of a table.</li><li>BIGSERIAL is the same idea with a much larger range, for tables that could reach billions of rows.</li></ul>\n<h3>Character types: VARCHAR, CHAR, TEXT</h3>\n<ul><li>VARCHAR(50) means variable-length text with a maximum of 50 characters. A 4-letter value only uses the space it needs.</li><li>CHAR(50) is fixed-length: it reserves room for 50 characters whatever the actual data length, so it is less efficient for varying text.</li><li>TEXT has no declared maximum and also uses only the space needed.</li><li>Older advice favoured VARCHAR with a limit for efficiency. Modern tests on very large databases show TEXT is only marginally slower or larger, and it avoids failures when a value turns out longer than expected.</li><li>Rule of thumb: use VARCHAR(n) when you truly know the maximum size; use TEXT when you want flexibility. Both are acceptable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Numbers and booleans</h3>\n<ul><li>INT stores whole numbers (age).</li><li>BOOLEAN stores true or false, suited to yes/no fields such as is_cool.</li><li>The Postgres documentation lists many more types (dates, character varying and others) and is worth skimming.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The friends table as created in Postgres</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE friends (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(50),\n  age INT,\n  is_cool BOOLEAN\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Inserting without an id; SERIAL fills it in</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">INSERT INTO friends (name, age, is_cool) VALUES ('Jack', 20, true);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using a non-unique field such as name to identify a row; use the primary key.</li><li>Declaring CHAR(n) when values vary in length (wasted space, and fixed-width padding).</li><li>Setting VARCHAR limits too small and later hitting values that do not fit.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l251", "<p class=\"summary\">Windows does not render flag emoji as images, so flags appear as two-letter country codes in VS Code and pgAdmin. This only matters for the flag quiz project in the browser, where Firefox shows them correctly.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>On Windows, flag emoji show as letter codes in VS Code, pgAdmin and Chrome. Data in .csv files and the database is still correct.</li><li>What matters for the project is how flags render in the browser page, not in the editor or pgAdmin.</li><li>Workaround for Windows users on the flag quiz: view the app in Firefox, which draws flag emoji.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The notes are based on the lesson text as mirrored in the reference repo, since the article could not be read inline.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Assuming the data is broken when you only see country codes instead of flags.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l252", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through pgAdmin: logging in to the server, creating a database named world, writing CREATE TABLE in the Query Tool, and importing a CSV into the new table. The exercise repeats the process for a flags table with emoji data.</p>\n<h3>Reading the CSV before designing the table</h3>\n<ul><li>CSV (comma separated values) holds one record per line with fields split by commas. The first line is a header naming the fields.</li><li>capitals.csv has the fields id, country, capital for every country. flags.csv has id, name, flag.</li><li>Choose column types by inspecting the data: id is unique so it becomes the primary key; country and capital are text with every value under 45 characters, so VARCHAR(45) fits.</li><li>Flags are emoji, which are UTF-8 text. They can be stored as VARCHAR, but TEXT is recommended here for UTF-8 handling and for practice with another type.</li></ul>\n<h3>pgAdmin set-up flow</h3>\n<ul><li>First launch may ask for a pgAdmin master password. This protects pgAdmin itself and is separate from the Postgres password, so record both.</li><li>Expand Servers and double-click the PostgreSQL server. Enter the password chosen at install time (user is postgres).</li><li>Under Databases there is a default postgres database. Create your own instead: right-click Databases, Create Database, name it world, keep owner postgres, Save.</li><li>Expand world, Schemas, Tables to confirm it has no tables yet.</li><li>With world selected, open the Query Tool; this is where SQL is written and run with the Run button.</li></ul>\n<h3>Writing CREATE TABLE</h3>\n<ul><li>Keywords are syntax highlighted, which helps spot typos such as a misspelled TABLE.</li><li>End every SQL statement with a semicolon; SQL, unlike JavaScript, needs it.</li><li>id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY: use SERIAL for primary keys as habit, even when importing existing ids, so later inserts get the next id automatically.</li><li>PRIMARY KEY promises each value in the column is unique per record. Other constraints (UNIQUE, NOT NULL) come in later lessons.</li><li>Separate fields with commas but leave no comma after the last field, or Postgres reports a syntax error.</li><li>A green 'Query returned successfully' message means the table exists. Right-click Tables and Refresh to see it in the tree.</li></ul>\n<h3>Viewing data and the SQL pgAdmin generates</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click the table, View/Edit Data, All Rows: pgAdmin writes a SELECT * FROM public.capitals ORDER BY id ASC query for you in the Query Tool.</li><li>The First 100 Rows option adds LIMIT 100 to the same query. Seeing this generated SQL is a quick way to learn syntax.</li><li>pgAdmin's GUI is recommended over using Postgres in the terminal.</li></ul>\n<h3>Importing a CSV</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click the table, Import/Export Data. The dialog often defaults to Export, so switch it to Import.</li><li>Pick the file with the folder icon and leave format as CSV.</li><li>In Options turn Header on, so the first line is treated as column names and not as a record.</li><li>On the Columns tab the header names should appear and must match the table's column names exactly (including case). If they differ, rename the table columns (table Properties, Columns tab, Save) or edit the CSV, then import again.</li><li>Click OK; green notifications with 'process completed' mean success. Refresh and View All Rows to check the data.</li><li>Check the file path shows the right CSV when importing a second file; the wrong file shape causes an error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Table creation via right-click Tables, Create, Table also works, but typing SQL is quicker and better practice.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Capitals table (created in the demo)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE capitals (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  country VARCHAR(45),\n  capital VARCHAR(45)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Flags table (the exercise solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE flags (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(45),\n  flag TEXT\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">SQL pgAdmin writes when viewing all rows</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM public.capitals\nORDER BY id ASC;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create and import the flags table</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The world database and capitals table already exist with data. Download flags.csv (250 rows, columns id, name, flag; flag holds emoji).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have a flags table in world populated with every country and its flag emoji.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open flags.csv in VS Code and note the header and which types each column needs.</li><li>In pgAdmin select the world database and open the Query Tool.</li><li>Write CREATE TABLE flags with id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(45), flag TEXT, ending in a semicolon with no trailing comma.</li><li>Run it, then refresh Tables to see flags.</li><li>Right-click flags, Import/Export Data, set Import, choose flags.csv, keep CSV format and enable Header.</li><li>Check the Columns tab lists id, name and flag, then click OK.</li><li>View All Rows and confirm the data and flag emoji.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A flags table with 250 rows; flags show as emoji (as country letters on Windows, which is a font limitation).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE flags (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(45),\n  flag TEXT\n);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Trailing comma after the last column definition, or a missing semicolon.</li><li>Leaving Import/Export on Export, or forgetting to tick Header (the header line then fails or becomes a data row).</li><li>CSV header names not matching table column names.</li><li>Mixing up the pgAdmin master password and the Postgres user password.</li><li>Importing the wrong CSV into a table because the old path is still set.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l253", "<p class=\"summary\">Connects a Node/Express app to the Postgres world database with the pg package and reads rows with SELECT * so a capitals quiz uses real data instead of a hard-coded array. The exercise repeats it for a flag quiz using the flags table.</p>\n<h3>The project: capitals quiz</h3>\n<ul><li>The app shows a country, the player types its capital, and the answer is compared with the database value. A correct answer raises the score; the first wrong answer ends the game with an alert showing the final score.</li><li>Comparison is normalised: the input is trimmed (leading/trailing spaces would break equality) and both strings are lower-cased before ===.</li><li>Starter code (from the download) already holds the Express, EJS and body-parser logic. Its quiz variable is a hard-coded array of three objects (France, US, UK) with country and capital keys, so refreshing repeats the same questions. The only new work is replacing that array with database rows.</li></ul>\n<h3>Existing app logic to understand first</h3>\n<ul><li>express.static('public') serves main.css and the background image; body-parser's urlencoded middleware parses the form body into req.body.</li><li>index.ejs shows the score, prints question.country in the h1, and has a text input named answer. autofocus selects the field on load and autocomplete='off' stops past-entry suggestions.</li><li>The form posts to /submit. The route reads req.body.answer, trims it, compares to currentQuestion.capital, bumps totalCorrect when equal, calls nextQuestion(), then renders index.ejs with question, wasCorrect and totalScore.</li><li>nextQuestion() picks a random element: quiz[Math.floor(Math.random() * quiz.length)].</li><li>GET / resets totalCorrect to 0 and picks a first question. The EJS page uses a small inline script: if wasCorrect is the string 'false' it fires the game-over alert and swaps the page for a restart link.</li></ul>\n<h3>Connecting to Postgres with pg</h3>\n<ul><li>Install with npm i pg in the project folder, then import pg from 'pg' (the project is ES modules, so package.json has type: module).</li><li>Create a client: new pg.Client({ user, host, database, password, port }). user defaults to postgres, host is localhost for a local DB, database is world, password is the one set at install, and port is 5432 by default.</li><li>Password matters little locally but is essential once a database is on the internet.</li><li>db.connect() opens the connection once at startup.</li><li>db.query('SELECT * FROM capitals', callback) runs the SQL. The callback gets (err, res); on error log err.stack, otherwise store res.rows (an array of objects keyed by column name) in quiz.</li><li>db.end() inside the callback closes the connection after the data is loaded. Closing is good practice so the session cannot do anything else by accident.</li><li>Because the quiz is fetched once at server start, the rest of the code is unchanged: it already works on a quiz array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Gotchas for the READ lesson</h3>\n<ul><li>Row objects use the column names from the table, so the capitals rows have id, country, capital and the flags rows have id, name, flag. The flags quiz must use currentQuestion.name for the answer and question.flag for display.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The query result is only populated after the callback runs; routes that fire before that would see an empty quiz. This works in practice because loading is quick.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Calling db.end() means the client cannot be reused for later queries; lessons after this one keep the connection open instead.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core pg wiring added to index.js</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;world&quot;,\n  password: &quot;YOUR_PASSWORD&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\nlet quiz = [];\ndb.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM capitals&quot;, (err, res) =&gt; {\n  if (err) {\n    console.error(&quot;Error executing query&quot;, err.stack);\n  } else {\n    quiz = res.rows; // [{ id: 1, country: 'Afghanistan', capital: 'Kabul' }, ...]\n  }\n  db.end();\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Random question and answer check (unchanged from the starter)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">async function nextQuestion() {\n  const randomCountry = quiz[Math.floor(Math.random() * quiz.length)];\n  currentQuestion = randomCountry;\n}\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  let answer = req.body.answer.trim();\n  let isCorrect = false;\n  if (currentQuestion.capital.toLowerCase() === answer.toLowerCase()) {\n    totalCorrect++;\n    isCorrect = true;\n  }\n  nextQuestion();\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { question: currentQuestion, wasCorrect: isCorrect, totalScore: totalCorrect });\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Postgres READ: flag quiz</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download 'Postgres READ' project. It is a flag quiz with an Express server, EJS page and CSS, but no database connection. Run npm install first. A working solution.js is included to try the finished game (nodemon solution.js).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Use the pg package and the flags table in the world database so index.js powers a quiz that shows a flag emoji and asks for the country name.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project folder and run npm install, then npm i pg if it is not already in package.json.</li><li>Run nodemon solution.js and play it at localhost:3000 to see the target behaviour.</li><li>Open index.js and look at the data: columns are id, name, flag (name, not country).</li><li>import pg from 'pg' and create a new pg.Client with user postgres, host localhost, database world, your password and port 5432.</li><li>Call db.connect().</li><li>Run db.query('SELECT * FROM flags', callback); on error log it, otherwise set quiz = res.rows; then call db.end().</li><li>Make sure answer checking uses currentQuestion.name and the EJS shows question.flag.</li><li>Run index.js with nodemon and check a new flag appears on refresh.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A flag quiz that picks a random flag from all 250 rows each round, accepts a country name (case and spaces ignored) and ends on the first wrong answer.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;world&quot;,\n  password: &quot;YOUR_PASSWORD&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\nlet quiz = [];\ndb.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM flags&quot;, (err, res) =&gt; {\n  if (err) {\n    console.error(&quot;Error executing query&quot;, err.stack);\n  } else {\n    quiz = res.rows;\n  }\n  db.end();\n});\n\n// in the POST /submit route:\nif (currentQuestion.name.toLowerCase() === answer.toLowerCase()) { /* score++ */ }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using the wrong column names (country/capital versus name/flag) for the flags table.</li><li>Database name or password not matching what was created in pgAdmin.</li><li>Forgetting npm install pg, or running as CommonJS when the project uses ES module imports.</li><li>Committing real passwords to Git (the reference repo does this).</li><li>On Windows, flags show as letters in the editor; view the quiz in Firefox.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l254", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds a world_food table in pgAdmin from a CSV, then works through progressively more selective SELECT queries: all rows, one column, several columns, WHERE with comparison operators, and pattern matching with LIKE, % and the || concatenation operator.</p>\n<h3>Setting up the practice data</h3>\n<ul><li>Dataset: world-food.csv, nine countries with rice and wheat production for 2021 in million metric tons. It lives in the existing database called world.</li><li>Table spec: id (SERIAL, PRIMARY KEY), country (VARCHAR(45)), rice_production (FLOAT), wheat_production (FLOAT). FLOAT is the new type here; it stores numbers with decimals.</li><li>Write the CREATE TABLE in the pgAdmin Query tool. Columns sit inside parentheses, separated by commas, with no comma after the last column, and the statement ends with a semicolon.</li><li>After running it, refresh the Tables node to see world_food. Viewing all rows at this point shows an empty grid.</li><li>Import: right-click the table, choose Import/Export Data, pick the CSV, keep Import (not Export) selected, and switch on Header in Options.</li><li>The CSV has no id column, so remove id from the 'Columns to import' list. SERIAL then generates ids 1 to 9 automatically.</li></ul>\n<h3>SELECT: choosing columns</h3>\n<ul><li>SELECT * FROM table returns every row and every column. The asterisk means 'all columns'.</li><li>SELECT column FROM table isolates one column, e.g. one phone-number column from a customers table.</li><li>SELECT col1, col2 FROM table returns several columns; separate the names with commas, no trailing comma before FROM.</li><li>The general shape so far: SELECT (what columns) FROM (which table).</li></ul>\n<h3>WHERE: filtering rows</h3>\n<ul><li>WHERE goes after the FROM clause and keeps only the rows whose condition is true.</li><li>Equality uses a single = sign (unlike JavaScript's == or ===). String values need single quotes in SQL: WHERE country = 'United States'.</li><li>The column you filter on does not have to be the column you select, so one query can look up one cell of data (e.g. a user's credit balance by username).</li><li>Comparison operators work as in other languages: &lt;, &gt;, &lt;=, &gt;=, plus = for equality.</li><li>Exercise results: US rice production is 8.7; countries with wheat_production &gt; 20 give a reduced list.</li></ul>\n<h3>LIKE: fuzzy matching</h3>\n<ul><li>= only matches an exact value, so 'United States' would miss a stored 'United States of America'. LIKE matches against a pattern instead.</li><li>% is the wildcard standing for any run of characters (including none).</li><li>|| is the string concatenation operator in Postgres, not a logical OR as in many languages. 'U' || '%' builds the pattern 'U%'.</li><li>'U%' matches values that start with U (Ukraine, United States). '%a' matches values ending in a (Australia, China, Ethiopia, India).</li><li>Writing the pattern as a literal ('U%') works the same. The || form matters later when the value is a variable or expression coming from backend code and must be joined to the wildcard.</li><li>Pattern matching is easy to get too broad: 'United%' would also match United Kingdom.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create the table</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE world_food (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  country VARCHAR(45),\n  rice_production FLOAT,\n  wheat_production FLOAT\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">SELECT variations</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM world_food;\nSELECT country FROM world_food;\nSELECT country, wheat_production FROM world_food;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">WHERE with equality and comparison</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT rice_production FROM world_food WHERE country = 'United States';\nSELECT country FROM world_food WHERE wheat_production &gt; 20;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">LIKE with wildcard and concatenation</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM world_food WHERE country LIKE 'U' || '%';\nSELECT * FROM world_food WHERE country LIKE '%' || 'a';\n-- equivalent literals: 'U%' and '%a'</pre>\n<h2>The task: World food queries</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> world-food.csv from the lesson resources and the existing world database in pgAdmin.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create and fill world_food, then write each query listed in the steps.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create world_food with id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, country VARCHAR(45), rice_production FLOAT, wheat_production FLOAT.</li><li>Import the CSV with Header on and the id column excluded; confirm nine rows.</li><li>Select all rows and columns.</li><li>Select only the country column.</li><li>Select country and wheat_production.</li><li>Get the rice production of the United States (single value, 8.7).</li><li>List countries whose wheat production is greater than 20.</li><li>List countries starting with U (Ukraine, United States) using LIKE and ||.</li><li>List countries ending in a (Australia, China, Ethiopia, India).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each query returns the reduced result grid described above.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT rice_production FROM world_food WHERE country = 'United States';\nSELECT country FROM world_food WHERE wheat_production &gt; 20;\nSELECT * FROM world_food WHERE country LIKE 'U' || '%';\nSELECT * FROM world_food WHERE country LIKE '%' || 'a';</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving id in the import column list when the CSV has no id values.</li><li>Trailing comma after the last column in CREATE TABLE.</li><li>Using double quotes around string values (Postgres treats them as identifiers); use single quotes.</li><li>Reading || as OR; it concatenates strings.</li><li>Using = when only part of the value is known.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l255", "<p class=\"summary\">Starts the Travel Tracker app: create a visited_countries table with NOT NULL and UNIQUE constraints, add rows by hand in pgAdmin, then make the Express route query the codes and colour the matching countries on an SVG world map.</p>\n<h3>App idea and starter project</h3>\n<ul><li>The app records countries you have visited, stores them in Postgres, and shades them on a world map. Part 1 only displays stored countries; adding new ones via the form is later.</li><li>Starter zip: unzip, then npm install. index.js is a bare Express skeleton. A finished version of this part is provided as solution1.js (run it with nodemon at localhost:3000 to see the target).</li><li>The world map is a huge inline SVG in index.ejs where each country is a path whose id is its two-letter ISO code. Collapse the ag-canvas section in the editor to make the file manageable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Table design: constraints</h3>\n<ul><li>Table visited_countries has two columns: id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY and country_code CHAR(2).</li><li>CHAR(2) holds exactly two characters, matching ISO country codes (FR, GB, US).</li><li>NOT NULL: the database rejects any row where country_code is missing, so no half-empty rows.</li><li>UNIQUE: the same code cannot be stored twice, so a country is never double counted.</li><li>NOT NULL and UNIQUE are two of the most common column modifiers; they push data validation into the database itself.</li></ul>\n<h3>Adding rows manually in pgAdmin</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the table's data grid and use the add-row button. Leave id empty so the SERIAL default fills it in.</li><li>country_code must be filled; saving a row with null fails because of NOT NULL.</li><li>Enter a few codes (e.g. FR, GB, US). Edits are not persisted until the Save data changes button is pressed; refreshing before saving discards them.</li><li>A rejected insert still consumes a serial value, so ids may skip numbers. That is fine because ids only need to be unique.</li></ul>\n<h3>Front end: how index.ejs uses the data</h3>\n<ul><li>EJS gets countries (an array of codes) and total (a count). Inside the script tag, the template output is inserted as text, so the browser receives a comma-joined string, not an array. Logging typeof confirms this.</li><li>Therefore the script calls .split(',') to rebuild an array of codes.</li><li>It then loops with forEach, finds each element with document.getElementById(code) and sets style.fill to teal.</li><li>total is printed in the heading 'Total Countries'.</li></ul>\n<h3>Back end solution walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Install the driver with npm i pg (already in the starter's dependencies) and import it.</li><li>Create a pg Client with user, host (localhost), database (world), password and port 5432, then call db.connect().</li><li>In the GET / handler (async), run await db.query('SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries'). This is the same SELECT column FROM table form from the previous lesson.</li><li>result.rows is an array of objects such as { country_code: 'FR' }. Log it to inspect the shape.</li><li>Loop over result.rows with forEach and push each row's country_code into a plain countries array.</li><li>Render index.ejs passing countries and total: countries.length.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference code calls db.end() at the end of the handler, which closes the connection after the first request, so a second page load fails. Later parts of the project drop it; keep the client open for the lifetime of the server.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create the table</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE visited_countries (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  country_code CHAR(2) NOT NULL UNIQUE\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.js solution for Part 1 (essential parts)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;world&quot;,\n  password: &quot;your_password&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries&quot;);\n  let countries = [];\n  result.rows.forEach((row) =&gt; {\n    countries.push(row.country_code);\n  });\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { countries: countries, total: countries.length });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Client-side colouring in index.ejs</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const country_codes = &quot;&lt;%= countries %&gt;&quot;.split(&quot;,&quot;);\ncountry_codes.forEach((code) =&gt; {\n  document.getElementById(code).style.fill = &quot;teal&quot;;\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Travel Tracker Part 1: display visited countries</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Travel Tracker starter with a skeleton index.js, index.ejs containing the SVG map and fill script, countries.csv, and a solution1.js to compare against.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create the table, add a few rows manually, and make the home route read them and render them coloured on the map with the correct total.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Unzip, npm install.</li><li>In pgAdmin create visited_countries with id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY and country_code CHAR(2) NOT NULL UNIQUE.</li><li>Add rows (e.g. FR, GB, US) and save.</li><li>Read index.ejs to see what it expects (countries and total).</li><li>In index.js import pg, configure the client and connect.</li><li>In the GET / route query country_code from visited_countries, log result.rows, flatten to an array of codes, and render with countries and total.</li><li>Run with nodemon, open localhost:3000, compare with solution1.js.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each stored country is filled teal on the map and the heading shows the number of rows.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries&quot;);\n  let countries = [];\n  result.rows.forEach((row) =&gt; countries.push(row.country_code));\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { countries: countries, total: countries.length });\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to click Save in the pgAdmin grid so rows vanish on refresh.</li><li>Assuming the EJS value is a real array in the browser; it is a string and needs split.</li><li>Lowercase or invalid codes make getElementById return null and the forEach throw.</li><li>Calling db.end() inside the route breaks later requests.</li><li>Using a different database name or password from your own setup.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l256", "<p class=\"summary\">Teaches INSERT INTO in SQL and parameterised queries with $1 placeholders in node-postgres, creates and imports a countries lookup table, and builds the POST /add route that converts a typed country name to its code and saves it.</p>\n<h3>INSERT INTO in SQL</h3>\n<ul><li>Form: INSERT INTO table (col1, col2, col3) VALUES (v1, v2, v3);</li><li>The column list order must match the order of the values. Leave out the id column so SERIAL generates it.</li><li>String values use single quotes; numbers are bare. End with a semicolon.</li><li>Exercise: insert a new world_food row for Italy with rice 1.46 and wheat 7.3, then view all rows to confirm it appears as row 10.</li><li>Inserting through SQL (rather than the pgAdmin grid) is what the app will need, because new data arrives from users at runtime.</li></ul>\n<h3>The countries lookup table</h3>\n<ul><li>Users type a country name, not a two-letter code, so the backend needs a name-to-code mapping. The countries table provides it (e.g. Angola maps to AO).</li><li>Columns: id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, country_code CHAR(2), country_name VARCHAR(100). 100 characters leaves room for long official names.</li><li>Create it in the world database, then import countries.csv via Import/Export Data with Header on. This CSV already contains ids, so import all three columns.</li><li>The result is a list of every country with its ISO code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Parameterised queries in node-postgres</h3>\n<ul><li>db.query(text, values) takes two arguments: the SQL string and an array of values.</li><li>In the SQL text use numbered placeholders $1, $2, $3. Each is replaced by the array item in that position.</li><li>This lets you pass variables or computed expressions (even 5*6) rather than hard-coded literals.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Because values are sent separately from the SQL, this also prevents SQL injection, so never build the query by concatenating user input into the string.</li></ul>\n<h3>POST /add solution</h3>\n<ul><li>The form in index.ejs posts to /add, so add app.post('/add', ...). body-parser is already set up, so the typed value is available as req.body['country'] (the input's name attribute is country).</li><li>First query: SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE country_name = $1 with [input].</li><li>result.rows is an array. If it is empty the name had no match; if rows.length !== 0, take rows[0].country_code.</li><li>Second query: INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code) VALUES ($1) with [countryCode].</li><li>Finish with res.redirect('/') so the home route re-queries and re-renders the map and total.</li><li>The visited-countries query is moved into a helper function (checkVisisted in the reference) returning the array of codes; this is optional but sets up reuse later.</li><li>Debug by console.log of result and result.rows to check the data's shape rather than hunting documentation.</li><li>Matching is exact on country_name, so entries must be spelled as in the table (Germany, Colombia work).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference solution does nothing when there is no match, so the request hangs with no response. Part 3 addresses error handling.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Insert a row by hand</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">INSERT INTO world_food (country, rice_production, wheat_production)\nVALUES ('Italy', 1.46, 7.3);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create the lookup table</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE countries (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  country_code CHAR(2),\n  country_name VARCHAR(100)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Placeholder syntax</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">db.query(\n  &quot;INSERT INTO world_food (country, rice_production, wheat_production) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)&quot;,\n  [&quot;Italy&quot;, 1.46, 7.3]\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core of solution2.js</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">async function checkVisited() {\n  const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries&quot;);\n  return result.rows.map((row) =&gt; row.country_code);\n}\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const countries = await checkVisited();\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { countries, total: countries.length });\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const input = req.body[&quot;country&quot;];\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE country_name = $1&quot;,\n    [input]\n  );\n  if (result.rows.length !== 0) {\n    const countryCode = result.rows[0].country_code;\n    await db.query(\n      &quot;INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code) VALUES ($1)&quot;,\n      [countryCode]\n    );\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Travel Tracker Part 2: add a country from the form</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Part 1 app, countries.csv in the lesson resources, and solution2.js for comparison.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Typing a country name in the form and pressing Add saves its code to visited_countries and the map shades it with an updated total.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Insert the Italy row into world_food using SQL.</li><li>Create the countries table and import countries.csv with all columns.</li><li>Check index.ejs: the form posts to /add and the input is named country.</li><li>Add an app.post('/add') route that reads req.body.country.</li><li>Look up the code in countries with a $1 placeholder.</li><li>If a row is found, INSERT the code into visited_countries.</li><li>Redirect to / so the map refreshes.</li><li>Test with names like Japan, Germany, Colombia.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each added country turns teal and the total increments.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const input = req.body[&quot;country&quot;];\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE country_name = $1&quot;,\n    [input]\n  );\n  if (result.rows.length !== 0) {\n    const countryCode = result.rows[0].country_code;\n    await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code) VALUES ($1)&quot;, [countryCode]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Column list and value list in a different order or of a different length.</li><li>Forgetting single quotes around string literals.</li><li>Not excluding the id when the CSV has none, or excluding it when the CSV (countries.csv) does have one.</li><li>Inserting a duplicate country violates UNIQUE and throws, which is unhandled here.</li><li>No response when the name is not found.</li><li>Case-sensitive exact matching, so 'germany' fails.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l257", "<p class=\"summary\">Optional polish for the Travel Tracker: wrap the queries in try/catch so bad or duplicate countries show an error in the input placeholder, and make the lookup forgiving with LOWER, LIKE and || so partial or differently cased names match.</p>\n<h3>Edge cases being fixed</h3>\n<ul><li>Unknown country name: Part 2 either hangs or crashes. Goal: re-show the page with the placeholder saying the country does not exist.</li><li>Duplicate country: inserting an already-saved code violates the UNIQUE constraint on country_code. Goal: placeholder says it was already added.</li><li>Long official names: Tanzania is stored under a longer official name, so exact matching fails even though the country is valid.</li><li>These are optional; solution3.js covers error handling and solution4.js adds the fuzzy lookup.</li></ul>\n<h3>Error handling with try/catch (solution3)</h3>\n<ul><li>index.ejs already shows the placeholder as the error value if an error variable exists (locals.error), otherwise the default prompt. So the server only needs to pass error.</li><li>Outer try wraps the lookup SELECT from countries and reading rows[0].country_code. If no row exists, rows[0] is undefined and reading its property throws, which lands in the outer catch.</li><li>Outer catch: log the error, fetch visited countries with the helper function, and res.render index.ejs with countries, total and error 'Country name does not exist, try again.'</li><li>Inner try wraps the INSERT into visited_countries. A UNIQUE violation throws and the inner catch renders the page with an 'already added' message.</li><li>In the error path use res.render, not res.redirect, because the error value has to be passed to the template; a redirect would drop it.</li><li>The helper that returns the visited codes is reused here so the map and total still display correctly alongside the error.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fuzzy, case-insensitive lookup (solution4)</h3>\n<ul><li>Replace the equality check with LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%'. The || joins wildcards to both sides of the placeholder, so the input may appear anywhere in the stored name.</li><li>Pass input.toLowerCase() as the parameter, and wrap the column in SQL LOWER() so both sides are lowercase and casing is irrelevant.</li><li>This is the reason to know the || form from the LIKE lesson: the pattern includes a variable that is not a literal.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A short input such as 'a' or 'in' will now match many countries and only the first row is used, so results can be surprising; ordering or an exact-match-first query would be a further refinement.</li><li>General lesson: find edge cases while testing, then fix them one at a time with more precise SQL and better error handling.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">solution3: nested try/catch in POST /add</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const input = req.body[&quot;country&quot;];\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(\n      &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE country_name = $1&quot;,\n      [input]\n    );\n    const countryCode = result.rows[0].country_code;\n    try {\n      await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code) VALUES ($1)&quot;, [countryCode]);\n      res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.log(err);\n      const countries = await checkVisited();\n      res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n        countries,\n        total: countries.length,\n        error: &quot;Country has already been added, try again.&quot;,\n      });\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n    const countries = await checkVisited();\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n      countries,\n      total: countries.length,\n      error: &quot;Country name does not exist, try again.&quot;,\n    });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">solution4: the only change, a fuzzy lowercase lookup</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const result = await db.query(\n  &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%';&quot;,\n  [input.toLowerCase()]\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Placeholder in index.ejs</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;country&quot; autofocus\n  placeholder=&quot;&lt;%= locals.error ? error : 'Enter country name' %&gt;&quot;&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Travel Tracker Part 3: error handling and fuzzy matching (optional)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The working Part 2 app (solution2.js), plus solution3.js and solution4.js to compare.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show a helpful message for unknown or duplicate countries, and let Tanzania (or a lowercase name) match the stored value.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Wrap the lookup query in try/catch; in the catch, log, fetch visited countries and render index.ejs with an error 'does not exist'.</li><li>Wrap the INSERT in its own try/catch; in the catch render with an 'already added' error.</li><li>Test by entering a nonsense name and re-adding a visited country; the input placeholder should change.</li><li>Change the lookup to LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%' and pass input.toLowerCase().</li><li>Test with Tanzania and with lowercase input.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Bad or duplicate entries leave the map intact and show a message; Tanzania is accepted.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const result = await db.query(\n  &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%';&quot;,\n  [input.toLowerCase()]\n);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using res.redirect on error, which loses the error value.</li><li>Forgetting that rows[0] is undefined for no match, so the thrown TypeError is what triggers the catch.</li><li>Passing the original-case input while lowercasing only one side of the comparison.</li><li>Leaving out the % wildcards so LIKE behaves like equality.</li><li>Very short inputs matching unintended countries.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l258", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces relational design in Postgres through the simplest relationship, one to one, using student and contact_detail tables linked by a primary key / foreign key pair. Shows how an INNER JOIN with ON combines the two tables into one wide result.</p>\n<h3>Why relationships matter</h3>\n<ul><li>Linking tables is the main strength of SQL databases; the next three lessons cover one to one, one to many and many to many.</li><li>Relationships are usually planned on a crow's-foot (ER) diagram first. Free tools like draw.io let you draw tables, fields and the links between them.</li></ul>\n<h3>One to one relationship</h3>\n<ul><li>One row in table A maps to at most one row in table B. Here one student has one contact_detail row.</li><li>Common motive is modularity: databases cope well with very many rows but get unwieldy with very many columns, so rarely-used or grouped columns can be split into a second table.</li><li>The student table holds first_name, last_name and an id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY (auto-incrementing from 1). contact_detail holds tel and address (e.g. for contacting parents).</li></ul>\n<h3>Linking with a foreign key</h3>\n<ul><li>contact_detail.id is declared INTEGER (it must match the type of the student primary key) and REFERENCES student(id). REFERENCES marks the column as a FOREIGN KEY pointing at student's primary key.</li><li>UNIQUE on that column stops the same student id appearing twice in contact_detail, which is what enforces the one-to-one rule (without it you would get one to many).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Postgres also rejects inserting a contact_detail row whose id does not exist in student, which is referential integrity.</li><li>The PRIMARY KEY uniquely identifies rows within its own table; the FOREIGN KEY identifies which row in another table a record belongs to.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inserting related data</h3>\n<ul><li>Insert the student first. Because id is SERIAL, the first student gets id 1.</li><li>Then insert into contact_detail using that same value (1) as the foreign key, plus a made-up phone and address.</li><li>Insert order matters: the referenced row must exist before the row that points at it.</li></ul>\n<h3>INNER JOIN</h3>\n<ul><li>SELECT * FROM student JOIN contact_detail ON student.id = contact_detail.id returns one wide row combining both tables' columns.</li><li>JOIN on its own means INNER JOIN: only rows with a match on both sides appear.</li><li>ON states the matching rule, here primary key equals foreign key. Table-qualified names (student.id) are needed because both tables have a column called id.</li><li>The result has columns from student first, then contact_detail, so id shows twice.</li></ul>\n<h3>SQL comments and setup</h3>\n<ul><li>SQL comments start with two dashes (--) and are ignored when run, used to label sections in queries.sql.</li><li>Download the lesson resources (Family Travel Tracker starter) which include queries.sql with all the lesson's statements, to copy rather than retype.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">One to one tables, data and inner join</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE student (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  first_name TEXT,\n  last_name TEXT\n);\n\n-- One to One\nCREATE TABLE contact_detail (\n  id INTEGER REFERENCES student(id) UNIQUE,\n  tel TEXT,\n  address TEXT\n);\n\nINSERT INTO student (first_name, last_name) VALUES ('Angela', 'Yu');\nINSERT INTO contact_detail (id, tel, address)\nVALUES (1, '+123456789', '123 App Brewery Road');\n\nSELECT *\nFROM student\nJOIN contact_detail\nON student.id = contact_detail.id;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Build the school database and run the one-to-one join</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Resources zip containing queries.sql with all statements for the next three lessons; pgAdmin connected to your server.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a school database, build the student and contact_detail tables, insert linked data and join them.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In pgAdmin collapse existing databases, create a new database named school and save.</li><li>Open the Query Tool on school.</li><li>Paste and run the two CREATE TABLE statements; refresh Schemas &gt; Tables to confirm both exist.</li><li>Run the INSERT statements from the Data section: first the student, then the contact_detail row using id 1.</li><li>Run the SELECT ... JOIN ... ON query and confirm one combined row with all five fields appears.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A single result row: id, first_name, last_name, id, tel, address for Angela Yu.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT *\nFROM student\nJOIN contact_detail\nON student.id = contact_detail.id</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting UNIQUE on the foreign key silently allows many contact rows per student.</li><li>Mismatched column types between primary and foreign key.</li><li>Inserting the child row before the parent exists.</li><li>Running the join in the wrong database (check the Query Tool is on school).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l259", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the one to many (equivalently many to one) relationship using student and homework_submission tables, where the 'many' table carries a foreign key to the 'one' table. Shows joining the two and narrowing the selected columns.</p>\n<h3>One to many vs many to one</h3>\n<ul><li>One row on one side relates to many rows on the other: one student has many homework submissions; one customer has many orders.</li><li>The direction depends on the starting table: from student it is one to many; from homework_submission it is many to one. It is the same relationship.</li><li>Each submission belongs to exactly one student (no group work in this example).</li><li>In crow's-foot diagrams the 'many' end has the three-pronged foot touching the foreign key field, the 'one' end attaches to the primary key.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where the keys live</h3>\n<ul><li>The many-side table gets its own primary key (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY) because every submission must be uniquely identifiable.</li><li>It also gets a foreign key column (student_id) storing the id of the owning student. No UNIQUE here, so the same student id can repeat, which is what makes it one to many.</li><li>Compared with one to one: the only structural difference is dropping UNIQUE and giving the child table its own id.</li></ul>\n<h3>Creating the table</h3>\n<ul><li>homework_submission has three fields: id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, mark INTEGER, student_id INTEGER REFERENCES student(id).</li><li>student_id is INTEGER so it matches the type of student.id; REFERENCES student(id) names the table and column it points to and makes it a foreign key.</li><li>Real schemas would add more columns (date, class, teacher); this one is kept minimal.</li></ul>\n<h3>Naming convention</h3>\n<ul><li>Singular (student) or plural (students) table names are both acceptable. Follow the team's rule and stay consistent, never mix them.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inserting data</h3>\n<ul><li>Only mark and student_id are supplied; id is auto-generated 1, 2, 3 by SERIAL.</li><li>One INSERT can add several rows by listing multiple value tuples separated by commas: (98, 1), (87, 1), (88, 1). All three belong to student 1.</li><li>Viewing homework_submission alone shows only numeric student ids, which are hard to read, hence the join.</li></ul>\n<h3>Joining</h3>\n<ul><li>SELECT * FROM student JOIN homework_submission ON student.id = homework_submission.student_id returns one row per submission with the student's details repeated on each.</li><li>Qualifying the column (table.column) is clearer; unqualified student_id works only because the name exists in just one table.</li><li>Narrow the output by listing columns instead of *: SELECT student.id, first_name, last_name, mark. id must be qualified because both tables have it; the other names are unique so need no prefix.</li><li>Table order after FROM does not change the matched set for an inner join.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Many-to-one table, data and joins</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE homework_submission (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  mark INTEGER,\n  student_id INTEGER REFERENCES student(id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO homework_submission (mark, student_id)\nVALUES (98, 1), (87, 1), (88, 1);\n\nSELECT *\nFROM student\nJOIN homework_submission\nON student.id = homework_submission.student_id;\n\nSELECT student.id, first_name, last_name, mark\nFROM student\nJOIN homework_submission\nON student.id = student_id;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add homework_submission and write the join yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> school database from the previous lesson with student and contact_detail; queries.sql from the resources.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create the many-side table, insert three submissions for student 1, then write the join before looking at the answer.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run CREATE TABLE homework_submission and refresh to see it.</li><li>Run the INSERT with three (mark, student_id) tuples.</li><li>View the table to see the raw ids.</li><li>Write a query joining student to homework_submission on the matching id, without peeking at queries.sql.</li><li>Narrow it to student.id, first_name, last_name and mark.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three rows, one per submission, each showing Angela Yu with marks 98, 87 and 88.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT student.id, first_name, last_name, mark\nFROM student\nJOIN homework_submission\nON student.id = student_id</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Adding UNIQUE to the foreign key turns it into one to one.</li><li>Selecting id without a table prefix gives an ambiguous column error.</li><li>Inventing a student_id that does not exist in student causes a foreign key violation.</li><li>Mixing singular and plural table names.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l260", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains many to many relationships as two one-to-many links joined through a third (junction) table, builds student/class/enrollment, joins all three tables, and introduces aliases with AS for columns and tables.</p>\n<h3>What many to many means</h3>\n<ul><li>A student takes many classes, and a class has many students. Two one-to-many relationships facing each other give many to many.</li><li>It is rarer and more complex than the other types.</li><li>A single foreign key cannot model it, so Postgres designs use a third table (a junction or link table) holding one row per pairing.</li></ul>\n<h3>The enrollment junction table</h3>\n<ul><li>Each enrollment row has a student_id and a class_id, e.g. student 1 in class 1, student 1 in class 3, student 2 in class 1.</li><li>In a design diagram this appears as student (one) to enrollment (many) and class (one) to enrollment (many), rather than one direct many-to-many line.</li><li>enrollment declares student_id INTEGER REFERENCES student(id) and class_id INTEGER REFERENCES class(id): both are foreign keys.</li><li>PRIMARY KEY (student_id, class_id) is a composite key: the pair is unique, so the same student cannot be enrolled in the same class twice. No separate id column is needed.</li><li>class table: id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, title VARCHAR(45) (a string capped at 45 characters).</li></ul>\n<h3>Sample data</h3>\n<ul><li>Add a second student (Jack Bauer, id 2) and three classes: English Literature (1), Maths (2), Physics (3), ids assigned in insertion order by SERIAL.</li><li>Enroll student 1 in classes 1 and 2, and student 2 in classes 2 and 3. Two separate INSERTs are used for readability but one could hold all four tuples.</li></ul>\n<h3>Joining three tables</h3>\n<ul><li>Start FROM enrollment, then chain two JOINs: student ON student.id = enrollment.student_id, and class ON class.id = enrollment.class_id.</li><li>Result has one row per enrollment record, with student and class details repeated as needed.</li><li>Such a table can answer questions: how many students take maths (2), which classes one student takes, which classes two students share.</li></ul>\n<h3>Aliases</h3>\n<ul><li>AS renames an output column: student.id AS stud makes that column header stud. Useful when several tables each have an id column.</li><li>Tables can also be aliased: FROM enrollment AS e JOIN student AS s ..., then use s.id and e.student_id in place of the full names. This shortens long queries.</li><li>The AS keyword is optional for tables: FROM enrollment e works the same.</li><li>Recommendation given: keep AS for column aliases in SELECT for readability, even though it is optional.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Once a table is aliased, the original name can no longer be used for it in that query.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Many to many tables, joins and aliases</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE class (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(45)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE enrollment (\n  student_id INTEGER REFERENCES student(id),\n  class_id INTEGER REFERENCES class(id),\n  PRIMARY KEY (student_id, class_id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO student (first_name, last_name) VALUES ('Jack', 'Bauer');\nINSERT INTO class (title) VALUES ('English Literature'), ('Maths'), ('Physics');\nINSERT INTO enrollment (student_id, class_id) VALUES (1, 1), (1, 2);\nINSERT INTO enrollment (student_id, class_id) VALUES (2, 2), (2, 3);\n\n-- Double join\nSELECT *\nFROM enrollment\nJOIN student ON student.id = enrollment.student_id\nJOIN class ON class.id = enrollment.class_id;\n\n-- Column alias\nSELECT student.id AS stud, first_name, last_name, title\nFROM enrollment\nJOIN student ON student.id = enrollment.student_id\nJOIN class ON class.id = enrollment.class_id;\n\n-- Table aliases (AS optional)\nSELECT s.id AS stud, first_name, last_name, title\nFROM enrollment e\nJOIN student s ON s.id = e.student_id\nJOIN class c ON c.id = e.class_id;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create the many-to-many tables and query them</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> school database with student, contact_detail and homework_submission tables.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Add class and enrollment, insert data, run the double join and experiment with aliases.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create the class and enrollment tables.</li><li>Insert Jack Bauer as a student, the three classes and the four enrollment rows.</li><li>Run the three-table join with SELECT *.</li><li>Change the select to use AS stud for the student id.</li><li>Rewrite the query with table aliases e, s and c, with and without AS.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Four rows, each pairing a student with a class; the aliased versions return identical data with a renamed id column.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT s.id AS id, first_name, last_name, title\nFROM enrollment e\nJOIN student s ON s.id = e.student_id\nJOIN class c ON c.id = e.class_id;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Inserting an enrollment pair twice violates the composite primary key.</li><li>Referencing a student or class id that does not exist fails the foreign key.</li><li>Using the full table name after aliasing it.</li><li>Ambiguous id column when selecting without a prefix.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l261", "<p class=\"summary\">Capstone for the relationships lessons: upgrade the Travel Tracker so several family members each have their own visited countries and map colour. Needs a users table, a one to many link from visited_countries, tab switching, a new-member form and per-user inserts. The second half walks through the solution.</p>\n<h3>What the finished app does</h3>\n<ul><li>Tabs across the top, one per family member, each tab tinted with that person's colour. Clicking a tab shows only that person's visited countries on the map and their total.</li><li>The Add button inserts a country for whichever user's tab is active, leaving other users unchanged.</li><li>An extra 'Add Family Member' tab opens a form for a name and a colour (radio buttons using CSS colour names); the new member becomes the active user.</li><li>Starter files supply index.ejs, new.ejs and CSS already working; you only need to pass a users array plus the other variables from index.js and write the missing routes. index.ejs may look red in the editor because of EJS tags inside attribute strings, but it is valid.</li></ul>\n<h3>Database design</h3>\n<ul><li>Relationship is one to many: one user, many visited countries.</li><li>users table: id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name (unique, not null) and color (a CSS colour name such as teal or powderblue).</li><li>visited_countries gets a user_id INTEGER column with REFERENCES users(id): a foreign key on the many side pointing to the one side's primary key.</li><li>Either ALTER the table or drop and recreate (DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visited_countries, users; or right-click delete in pgAdmin). queries.sql contains the setup plus seed rows; run it in the world database, not school.</li><li>Final SELECT * FROM visited_countries JOIN users ON users.id = user_id shows rows with repeating user details.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reading a user's countries (checkVisited)</h3>\n<ul><li>Old query selected every country_code; now it joins users and filters: ... JOIN users ON users.id = user_id WHERE user_id = $1 with [currentUserId] as the parameter.</li><li>The join isn't strictly required for the code list, but it shows the relationship; the WHERE is what narrows to one user.</li><li>currentUserId is a module-level variable defaulting to 1, so the first tab shows on first load.</li><li>Result rows are mapped to an array of two-letter codes; its length is the total passed to the view.</li></ul>\n<h3>Loading users and the current one</h3>\n<ul><li>getCurrentUser runs SELECT * FROM users, stores the rows into the users array (a list of objects with id, name, color), then returns the matching one with users.find.</li><li>Keeping a sample users array at the top documents the data shape.</li><li>Use loose == in the comparison, because an id submitted from a form arrives as a string, and strict === against a number would fail. Debug by logging typeof on both sides.</li><li>The / route renders index.ejs with countries, total, users and the current user's color.</li></ul>\n<h3>How index.ejs uses the data</h3>\n<ul><li>users.forEach emits one submit input and label per user: the input's name is user and its value is user.id, the label shows user.name and its background uses user.color.</li><li>The form posts to /user. A further submit button named add with value new leads to the new-member page.</li><li>The Add button background and the JavaScript that fills map paths use the same color variable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Routes</h3>\n<ul><li>POST /user: if req.body.add equals new, render new.ejs; otherwise set currentUserId = req.body.user and redirect to /.</li><li>POST /new: read name and color from the body, run INSERT INTO users (name, color) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *, take rows[0].id as the new currentUserId, redirect to /.</li><li>RETURNING * makes Postgres return the inserted row(s), which is how the app learns the auto-generated id.</li><li>POST /add: look up the country code as before, then INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code, user_id) VALUES ($1, $2) with currentUserId, then redirect.</li><li>Because / reloads data for currentUserId, redirecting after each change refreshes tabs, colours and map.</li></ul>\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul><li>Use VS Code's 'Select for Compare' then 'Compare with Selected' to diff index.js against solution.js.</li><li>Plan data shapes by writing them out; it prevents bugs.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The solution does not validate duplicate countries or empty names; the first catch only logs errors.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Database setup (queries.sql)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visited_countries, users;\n\nCREATE TABLE users(\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  name VARCHAR(15) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  color VARCHAR(15)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE visited_countries(\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  country_code CHAR(2) NOT NULL,\n  user_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO users (name, color)\nVALUES ('Angela', 'teal'), ('Jack', 'powderblue');\n\nINSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code, user_id)\nVALUES ('FR', 1), ('GB', 1), ('CA', 2), ('FR', 2);\n\nSELECT *\nFROM visited_countries\nJOIN users ON users.id = user_id;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Key server code (solution.js, condensed)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">let currentUserId = 1;\nlet users = [\n  { id: 1, name: &quot;Angela&quot;, color: &quot;teal&quot; },\n  { id: 2, name: &quot;Jack&quot;, color: &quot;powderblue&quot; },\n];\n\nasync function checkVisited() {\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries JOIN users ON users.id = user_id WHERE user_id = $1;&quot;,\n    [currentUserId]\n  );\n  return result.rows.map((row) =&gt; row.country_code);\n}\n\nasync function getCurrentUser() {\n  const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users&quot;);\n  users = result.rows;\n  return users.find((user) =&gt; user.id == currentUserId);\n}\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const countries = await checkVisited();\n  const currentUser = await getCurrentUser();\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n    countries, total: countries.length, users, color: currentUser.color,\n  });\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const input = req.body[&quot;country&quot;];\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(\n      &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%';&quot;,\n      [input.toLowerCase()]\n    );\n    const countryCode = result.rows[0].country_code;\n    await db.query(\n      &quot;INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code, user_id) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;,\n      [countryCode, currentUserId]\n    );\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) { console.log(err); }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/user&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.body.add === &quot;new&quot;) {\n    res.render(&quot;new.ejs&quot;);\n  } else {\n    currentUserId = req.body.user;\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/new&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const { name, color } = req.body;\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;INSERT INTO users (name, color) VALUES($1, $2) RETURNING *;&quot;,\n    [name, color]\n  );\n  currentUserId = result.rows[0].id;\n  res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Tab form in index.ejs</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;form action=&quot;/user&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;% users.forEach(function(user) { %&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;user&quot; value=&quot;&lt;%= user.id %&gt;&quot; id=&quot;&lt;%= user.id %&gt;&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;label for=&quot;&lt;%= user.id %&gt;&quot; style=&quot;background-color: &lt;%= user.color %&gt;;&quot;&gt;&lt;%= user.name %&gt;&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;% }); %&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;add&quot; value=&quot;new&quot; id=&quot;tab&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;tab&quot;&gt;Add Family Member&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Family Travel Tracker</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Family Travel Tracker.zip: previous Travel Tracker code, index.ejs and new.ejs ready, plus queries.sql containing the answer SQL at the bottom and solution.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Support multiple family members, each with a colour and their own visited countries, and let new members be added.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run nodemon on index.js and confirm the starter renders two tabs.</li><li>Design the users table (id, name, colour) and add a user_id foreign key to visited_countries, recreating the tables if needed; seed some data.</li><li>Make the home route pass users, countries, total and the current user's colour.</li><li>Change the countries query to filter by the current user id.</li><li>Add POST /user: show new.ejs when add is new, else switch currentUserId and redirect.</li><li>Add POST /new: insert the user with RETURNING *, make them current, redirect.</li><li>Change POST /add to insert with the current user's id.</li><li>Test: switch tabs, add a country for one user, add a new member and a country for them.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Tabs for each member in their colour, per-user maps and totals, and new members appearing with their own empty map.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">let currentUserId = 1;\nlet users = [\n  { id: 1, name: &quot;Angela&quot;, color: &quot;teal&quot; },\n  { id: 2, name: &quot;Jack&quot;, color: &quot;powderblue&quot; },\n];\n\nasync function checkVisited() {\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;SELECT country_code FROM visited_countries JOIN users ON users.id = user_id WHERE user_id = $1;&quot;,\n    [currentUserId]\n  );\n  return result.rows.map((row) =&gt; row.country_code);\n}\n\nasync function getCurrentUser() {\n  const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users&quot;);\n  users = result.rows;\n  return users.find((user) =&gt; user.id == currentUserId);\n}\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const countries = await checkVisited();\n  const currentUser = await getCurrentUser();\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n    countries, total: countries.length, users, color: currentUser.color,\n  });\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const input = req.body[&quot;country&quot;];\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(\n      &quot;SELECT country_code FROM countries WHERE LOWER(country_name) LIKE '%' || $1 || '%';&quot;,\n      [input.toLowerCase()]\n    );\n    const countryCode = result.rows[0].country_code;\n    await db.query(\n      &quot;INSERT INTO visited_countries (country_code, user_id) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;,\n      [countryCode, currentUserId]\n    );\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) { console.log(err); }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/user&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.body.add === &quot;new&quot;) {\n    res.render(&quot;new.ejs&quot;);\n  } else {\n    currentUserId = req.body.user;\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/new&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const { name, color } = req.body;\n  const result = await db.query(\n    &quot;INSERT INTO users (name, color) VALUES($1, $2) RETURNING *;&quot;,\n    [name, color]\n  );\n  currentUserId = result.rows[0].id;\n  res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using === when comparing ids from the form (strings) with numeric ids.</li><li>Running the setup SQL in the school database instead of world.</li><li>Forgetting user_id when inserting visited countries.</li><li>Not redirecting after POST /new so the new user is not current.</li><li>Reference code has a typo checkVisisted (normalised above) and a hard-coded password; use your own credentials.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l262", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the Postgres commands that change existing structure and data: ALTER TABLE (rename, change column type, add column, add constraint), DROP TABLE, UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE, ORDER BY with ASC/DESC, and DELETE FROM ... WHERE. Four short challenges run against the Family Travel Tracker tables in pgAdmin's Query Tool.</p>\n<h3>ALTER TABLE: changing the schema</h3>\n<ul><li>ALTER TABLE &lt;table&gt; &lt;action&gt; is the entry point for any structural change. The action that follows decides what happens.</li><li>Rename a table: ALTER TABLE student RENAME TO user (the example turns a student table into a user table).</li><li>Change a column's data type: ALTER TABLE &lt;table&gt; ALTER COLUMN &lt;column&gt; TYPE &lt;new type&gt;. This is a two-level ALTER (table, then column). Example idea: a TEXT column becomes VARCHAR(20).</li><li>Add a column: ALTER TABLE &lt;table&gt; ADD COLUMN &lt;name&gt; &lt;type&gt;. Example: a contact_detail table holding telephone and address gets a third column, email TEXT.</li><li>Add a constraint after the table exists: ALTER TABLE &lt;table&gt; ADD UNIQUE (col1, col2). The constraint is the same one you can declare at the end of CREATE TABLE.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Existing rows must already satisfy a new constraint, otherwise the ALTER fails. Dropping a column uses ALTER TABLE &lt;t&gt; DROP COLUMN &lt;c&gt;, which she mentions only in passing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Composite UNIQUE constraint</h3>\n<ul><li>UNIQUE over several columns makes the combination unique, not each column on its own. With UNIQUE (a, c), a row (a=1, c=1) can coexist with (a=1, c=2) but a second (1, 1) pair is rejected. The b column is not part of the check.</li><li>Why it matters in the Family Travel Tracker: visited_countries had country_code as UNIQUE, which is wrong once several family members can visit the same country. The real rule is that one user cannot have the same country twice, so the pair (user_id, country_code) must be unique.</li><li>After the constraint exists, inserting user_id 1 with country_code 'FR' again fails with a duplicate-key / unique-violation error in pgAdmin.</li><li>This error is useful for the app: the server can catch it and show the visitor a message about the duplicate, instead of silently storing the same data twice.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Postgres auto-names the constraint (e.g. visited_countries_user_id_country_code_key) unless you write ADD CONSTRAINT &lt;name&gt; UNIQUE (...).</li></ul>\n<h3>DROP TABLE</h3>\n<ul><li>DROP TABLE &lt;table&gt; removes the whole table and its rows from the database. It is the SQL equivalent of right-click then Delete on a table in pgAdmin. Be careful, there is no undo.</li><li>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS &lt;t1&gt;, &lt;t2&gt; only acts when the table is present, so the script does not error otherwise. The queries.sql file in the Family Travel Tracker starter starts with this so the script can be re-run and rebuild visited_countries and users from a clean state.</li></ul>\n<h3>UPDATE: change data in existing rows</h3>\n<ul><li>Pattern: UPDATE &lt;table&gt; SET &lt;column&gt; = &lt;new value&gt; WHERE &lt;condition&gt;.</li><li>The WHERE clause narrows which rows change. Without it every row in the table gets the new value, which is almost never intended. Filtering on the primary key (id = 1) targets exactly one row.</li><li>Different conditions touch different parts of the table, e.g. WHERE id = 3 or WHERE name = 'Jack'.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Several columns can be set at once with a comma list: SET name = 'A', color = 'red'.</li></ul>\n<h3>ORDER BY, ASC and DESC</h3>\n<ul><li>SELECT * FROM &lt;table&gt; ORDER BY &lt;column&gt; [ASC | DESC] returns rows sorted by that column. It changes only the order of the result, not the stored data.</li><li>ASC is ascending and is the default. Numbers go small to large, text goes A to Z. DESC reverses this (large to small, Z to A).</li><li>The sort column does not have to be the id, so names, dates, and so on all work.</li></ul>\n<h3>DELETE FROM: remove rows</h3>\n<ul><li>Table-level removal uses DROP; row-level removal uses DELETE. Pattern: DELETE FROM &lt;table&gt; WHERE &lt;condition&gt;.</li><li>As with UPDATE, leaving out WHERE would remove every row (the table itself stays).</li><li>Conditions can be combined with AND: WHERE user_id = 1 AND country_code = 'FR' removes only the row matching both parts.</li><li>Typical use case: a user wrongly recorded a country (user 2 and AU) so that row is deleted by its id.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">ALTER TABLE variants</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">ALTER TABLE student RENAME TO &quot;user&quot;;\nALTER TABLE contact_detail ALTER COLUMN telephone TYPE VARCHAR(20);\nALTER TABLE contact_detail ADD COLUMN email TEXT;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Composite unique constraint on an existing table (challenge solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">ALTER TABLE visited_countries\nADD UNIQUE (user_id, country_code);\n\n-- Test: second run fails with a unique violation\nINSERT INTO visited_countries (user_id, country_code) VALUES (1, 'FR');</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Safe drop</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">DROP TABLE IF EXISTS visited_countries, users;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">UPDATE challenge solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">UPDATE users\nSET name = 'Angelina'\nWHERE id = 1;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">ORDER BY challenge solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY name DESC;\n-- numeric example\nSELECT * FROM users ORDER BY id ASC;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">DELETE by id, and by two conditions</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">DELETE FROM visited_countries WHERE id = 6;\n\nDELETE FROM visited_countries\nWHERE user_id = 1 AND country_code = 'FR';</pre>\n<h2>The task: Four short pgAdmin Query Tool challenges on the Family Travel Tracker database</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The users and visited_countries tables from the Family Travel Tracker lesson, with some rows (at least a couple of users, and visited countries for each). Work in pgAdmin: select the table, open the Query Tool, run SQL there instead of using the GUI.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Practise the schema change (ALTER), the data change (UPDATE), sorting (ORDER BY) and row removal (DELETE) with SQL.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Challenge 1: add a constraint so that user_id and country_code are unique together in visited_countries. Use ALTER TABLE ... ADD UNIQUE (user_id, country_code). Then try inserting a duplicate pair (user 1, 'FR') and confirm the unique-violation error.</li><li>Challenge 2: look at the users table, then run an UPDATE that sets the name of the user with id 1 to 'Angelina' using SET and WHERE id = 1. View the table to confirm only that row changed.</li><li>Challenge 3: select everything from users, sorted by the name column in descending order (ORDER BY name DESC). Add a couple of users first if the table is nearly empty.</li><li>Challenge 4: pick a row in visited_countries, note its id, user_id and country_code, and delete it with DELETE FROM visited_countries WHERE id = &lt;n&gt;. Then delete another row using a two-part condition (user_id and country_code joined with AND). Re-view the table after each.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The duplicate insert is rejected; user 1 now has the new name; the SELECT returns names in reverse alphabetical order; the chosen visited_countries rows disappear from the table.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">ALTER TABLE visited_countries ADD UNIQUE (user_id, country_code);\n\nUPDATE users SET name = 'Angelina' WHERE id = 1;\n\nSELECT * FROM users ORDER BY name DESC;\n\nDELETE FROM visited_countries WHERE id = 6;\nDELETE FROM visited_countries WHERE user_id = 1 AND country_code = 'FR';</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting WHERE on UPDATE or DELETE affects every row in the table.</li><li>Adding a UNIQUE constraint to a table that already holds duplicate pairs fails until the duplicates are removed.</li><li>Keeping UNIQUE on country_code alone in a multi-user table blocks two users from visiting the same country.</li><li>DROP TABLE is irreversible and removes data, so prefer IF EXISTS in scripts and double-check the name.</li><li>Sorting text with ORDER BY is alphabetical, so mixed-case or numeric-looking text may not sort as expected (extra note).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l263", "<p class=\"summary\">Guided project: turn an in-memory to-do list (Express, EJS, body-parser) into a persistent one backed by a Postgres table called items, implementing all four CRUD operations with the pg package. The lecture explains the starter code, sets the brief, then walks through the finished solution route by route.</p>\n<h3>What the project is and the starting point</h3>\n<ul><li>Permalist is a to-do list whose items survive refreshes and server restarts because they live in Postgres, not in a JavaScript array.</li><li>Download Permalist Project.zip from the lesson resources, extract it, and open it in VS Code. It already includes the styling, icons and EJS views (with header and footer partials).</li><li>Starter index.js keeps items in a plain array. Run it with npm i then nodemon index.js and open localhost:3000. Adding an item works, but restarting the server resets the array, because RAM-held data disappears with the process. That is the problem Postgres solves here.</li><li>In the starter, the /edit and /delete routes exist as forms in the front end but the server does nothing with them yet.</li><li>Suggested time: roughly one to two hours, with more thinking than typing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Understanding the front end before coding</h3>\n<ul><li>index.ejs loops over listItems and renders one row per item: a checkbox form (delete), a paragraph with the title, an edit form, and an edit button.</li><li>The delete form posts to /delete. Its checkbox has name deleteItemId and value set to the item's id, and an onchange handler that submits the form as soon as the box is ticked.</li><li>The edit form posts to /edit. It has two inputs: a hidden one named updatedItemId carrying the item's id, and a text input named updatedItemTitle prefilled with the current title via EJS, so the user edits rather than retypes.</li><li>The text input and the done button start hidden. A small browser script, handler(id), is called by the pencil button: it hides the paragraph and edit button and reveals the input and done button, so the title visually becomes an editable field. This script has nothing to do with persistence.</li><li>The add form has a text input named newItem and posts to /add.</li><li>body-parser (urlencoded, extended: true) turns these form fields into req.body.&lt;name&gt; on the server. Knowing exactly which name each form sends is the key to writing the routes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Database setup</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a database called permalist (pgAdmin: right-click Databases, Create, Database, Save).</li><li>Inside it create table items with id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY (auto-incrementing whole number that identifies each row) and title VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL (max 100 characters; NOT NULL blocks rows without a title, an optional safeguard for later error checking).</li><li>Optionally seed two rows matching the array in the starter (Buy milk, Finish homework). The table could equally start empty and be filled through the app.</li><li>queries.sql in the project holds this schema if stuck, but the suggestion is to attempt it from earlier lessons first.</li><li>The provided solution.js only runs if names match: database permalist, table items, columns id and title.</li></ul>\n<h3>Read: connect and select</h3>\n<ul><li>Import pg (already listed in package.json with body-parser, ejs and express) and create new pg.Client with user, host, database, password and port. Same settings as previous projects except database is now permalist; a wrong database name is the usual connection failure. Then call db.connect().</li><li>In app.get('/'), inside try/catch, await db.query('SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY id ASC'), assign result.rows to items, and pass it to res.render('index.ejs', { listTitle: 'Today', listItems: items }).</li><li>result.rows is an array of objects, one per row, with column names as keys (here id and title). Logging it with console.log is the recommended way to inspect it.</li><li>ORDER BY id ASC is optional but useful: without it Postgres may return an edited row at the end, so items jump around after an update. Sorting by id keeps the original order.</li><li>Error handling is basic in this project: log the error in catch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Create: the /add route</h3>\n<ul><li>The server reads the new text from req.body.newItem.</li><li>It runs db.query('INSERT INTO items (title) VALUES ($1)', [item]). The $1 placeholder is filled from the array in the second argument (parameterised query). id is generated by SERIAL, so only title is supplied.</li><li>After the insert, res.redirect('/'). The redirect makes the browser request the home route again, which re-queries the database and re-renders the page, effectively refreshing it with the new row.</li><li>Errors are caught and logged. The earlier items.push line from the in-memory version is no longer needed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Update: the /edit route</h3>\n<ul><li>Clicking the pencil swaps the paragraph for the prefilled input; submitting with the done button posts updatedItemTitle and updatedItemId to /edit.</li><li>The route reads both fields from req.body, then runs db.query('UPDATE items SET title = ($1) WHERE id = $2', [item, id]). Order of the values array must match the numbering: $1 is the new title, $2 the id.</li><li>The id travels through a hidden input because the browser shows only the title, but the server has to know which row to change.</li><li>Redirect to '/' afterwards, log any error.</li></ul>\n<h3>Delete: the /delete route</h3>\n<ul><li>Ticking the checkbox fires onchange, which submits its form, POSTing deleteItemId (the row's id) to /delete.</li><li>The route reads req.body.deleteItemId and runs db.query('DELETE FROM items WHERE id = $1', [id]), then redirects to '/'.</li><li>WHERE id = $1 is essential so only that row is removed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Upgrade ideas given</h3>\n<ul><li>Sort by creation date: add a column with ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN, store a date from JavaScript or the database, and show it.</li><li>Several lists (for example Today, This week, This month, or Work and School), which implies a lists table and a relationship between lists and items.</li><li>A family to-do list where different users own different chores, reusing the user pattern from the Family Travel Tracker.</li><li>The project is a common interview exercise, so adding a personal improvement helps it stand out.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Table setup (queries.sql)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE items (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL\n);\n\nINSERT INTO items (title) VALUES ('Buy milk'), ('Finish homework');</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished index.js (reference solution, tidied)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;permalist&quot;,\n  password: &quot;&lt;your password&gt;&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\nlet items = [];\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY id ASC&quot;);\n    items = result.rows;\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { listTitle: &quot;Today&quot;, listItems: items });\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const item = req.body.newItem;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO items (title) VALUES ($1)&quot;, [item]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/edit&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const item = req.body.updatedItemTitle;\n  const id = req.body.updatedItemId;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;UPDATE items SET title = ($1) WHERE id = $2&quot;, [item, id]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/delete&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = req.body.deleteItemId;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;DELETE FROM items WHERE id = $1&quot;, [id]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`));</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Key parts of views/index.ejs, simplified</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- list rendering: checkbox (delete), title paragraph, hidden edit form, edit button --&gt;\n&lt;% for (let item of listItems) { %&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;item&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;form action=&quot;/delete&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; onchange=&quot;this.form.submit()&quot;\n             name=&quot;deleteItemId&quot; value=&quot;&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n\n    &lt;p id=&quot;title&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot;&gt;&lt;%= item.title %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n    &lt;form class=&quot;edit&quot; action=&quot;/edit&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; name=&quot;updatedItemId&quot; value=&quot;&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;input&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;updatedItemTitle&quot;\n             value=&quot;&lt;%= item.title %&gt;&quot; autocomplete=&quot;off&quot; hidden&gt;\n      &lt;button id=&quot;done&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot; class=&quot;edit&quot; type=&quot;submit&quot; hidden&gt;done&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;button id=&quot;edit&lt;%= item.id %&gt;&quot; class=&quot;edit&quot;\n            onclick=&quot;handler('&lt;%= item.id %&gt;')&quot;&gt;edit&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;% } %&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- add form --&gt;\n&lt;form class=&quot;item&quot; action=&quot;/add&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;newItem&quot; placeholder=&quot;New Item&quot; autocomplete=&quot;off&quot; autofocus&gt;\n  &lt;button class=&quot;add&quot; type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;list&quot; value=&quot;&lt;%= listTitle %&gt;&quot;&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n\n&lt;script&gt;\n  function handler(id) {\n    document.getElementById(&quot;title&quot; + id).setAttribute(&quot;hidden&quot;, true);\n    document.getElementById(&quot;edit&quot; + id).setAttribute(&quot;hidden&quot;, true);\n    document.getElementById(&quot;done&quot; + id).removeAttribute(&quot;hidden&quot;);\n    document.getElementById(&quot;input&quot; + id).removeAttribute(&quot;hidden&quot;);\n  }\n&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Permalist: persist a to-do list in Postgres with full CRUD</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Permalist Project.zip: index.js (in-memory array version), views/index.ejs plus header/footer partials, public/styles and icon assets, package.json (express, ejs, body-parser, pg), and queries.sql. The page renders and adds items to the array, but edit and delete do nothing and data is lost on restart.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Replace the array with a Postgres database so items can be created, read, edited and deleted and the list survives refreshes and server restarts.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Read index.js and index.ejs first. Note the names each form sends: newItem (/add), updatedItemTitle plus updatedItemId (/edit), deleteItemId (/delete), and how the handler function toggles the edit UI.</li><li>Run npm i, then nodemon index.js, and check localhost:3000 works with the array. Restart the server to see the data reset.</li><li>In pgAdmin create database permalist.</li><li>Create table items with id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY and title VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL. Optionally insert 'Buy milk' and 'Finish homework'.</li><li>In index.js import pg, create a pg.Client configured for the permalist database, and call connect().</li><li>GET /: query all rows ordered by id, store result.rows in items, and render index.ejs with listTitle and listItems.</li><li>POST /add: insert req.body.newItem as a new title with a parameterised query, then redirect to '/'.</li><li>POST /edit: update title where id matches, using req.body.updatedItemTitle and req.body.updatedItemId, then redirect.</li><li>POST /delete: delete the row whose id equals req.body.deleteItemId, then redirect.</li><li>Test each action, restart the server and refresh to confirm the data persists. Optionally compare with solution.js, or attempt an upgrade.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A working to-do list: items listed from the database in id order, new items appear after adding, the pencil lets you rename an item and save it, ticking the checkbox removes it, and everything is still there after restarting the server.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;,\n  host: &quot;localhost&quot;,\n  database: &quot;permalist&quot;,\n  password: &quot;&lt;your password&gt;&quot;,\n  port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\nlet items = [];\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM items ORDER BY id ASC&quot;);\n    items = result.rows;\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { listTitle: &quot;Today&quot;, listItems: items });\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/add&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const item = req.body.newItem;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO items (title) VALUES ($1)&quot;, [item]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/edit&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const item = req.body.updatedItemTitle;\n  const id = req.body.updatedItemId;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;UPDATE items SET title = ($1) WHERE id = $2&quot;, [item, id]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/delete&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = req.body.deleteItemId;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;DELETE FROM items WHERE id = $1&quot;, [id]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong database name in the pg.Client config (still world or another earlier project) stops the app connecting.</li><li>The values array order must match $1, $2 in the UPDATE; swapping them writes the id into the title or updates nothing.</li><li>Forgetting res.redirect('/') leaves the request hanging, and the page does not refresh with the new data.</li><li>Without ORDER BY the list can reorder after an update.</li><li>Form field names in the EJS (newItem, updatedItemTitle, updatedItemId, deleteItemId) must match what the route reads from req.body.</li><li>Leaving out WHERE on the UPDATE or DELETE would change or remove every row.</li><li>Reference repo note: its index.js already contains the finished solution and is identical to solution.js, and it holds a hard-coded local password; use your own and keep it out of version control. (Extra)</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Only unhandled errors are logged, so a failed query leaves the browser waiting; add a res.status(500) response for production use.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l264", "<p class=\"summary\">A short motivational tip about building a daily routine to reduce decision fatigue and make learning to code a habit.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>A fixed daily routine reduces the number of decisions to make during the day, which frees energy for learning.</li><li>Start the day with a short physical activity such as a walk before sitting down to work.</li><li>Do the hardest or most-avoided learning task first, for example a difficult module or concept you keep postponing. Finishing it early gives a sense of achievement and makes the rest of the day easier.</li><li>For a multi-day task, aim for at least about an hour on it each day.</li><li>Design the morning and day so the routine runs automatically, building towards a daily coding habit.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Postponing the hardest topic until later in the day tends to mean it never gets done.</li></ul>");
